<%# ONE CATEGORY: a single quiet line unless something is wrong with it. Spec §4.3-4.4.
    Locals: `category`, `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    `data-expanded` is the auto-expand rule: anything needing attention opens itself, so
    trouble is never hidden behind a chevron. Both states render server-side.

    Status through `presenter.row_for`, never `category.status` — HoldingStatus and
    HoldingCalculator both default to Date.current, and a bare call here would compute against a
    different day than the rest of the screen, silently. The row carries the status AND both label
    suffixes as one object, which is what lets the status span be shared with the Budget page.

    THE `orphan` LOCAL IS GONE (Task 6) with the shape it described. A category belongs to no
    account and needs none — allocating money moves nothing physical (two-ledger spec §2) — so
    "nothing can fund this" has no cause left, and `Row#needs_attention?` is the status's own answer
    rather than an OR with a second one. %>
<% row = presenter.row_for(category) %>
<% problem = row.needs_attention? %>
<% rules = presenter.dated_rules_for(category) %>
<div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0"
     data-holding-name="<%= category.name %>"
     data-expanded="<%= problem %>">
  <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
    <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= category.name %></span>
    <%# THE STATUS SPAN IS SHARED WITH THE BUDGET PAGE (see `shared/_holding_status`). Both suffixes
        travel on `row` — through the presenter, never a bare `category.holding_calculator`, because
        a calculator or a clock built here would date itself against `Date.current` and disagree
        with the rest of the screen silently — and the partial threads them together, so this
        caller cannot pass one and drop the other. It did: for two of this plan's tasks Home's
        pools band and its attention band printed different suffixes for the same envelope inches
        apart. The date clause is Home's and rides on `Row#due_marker?`. %>
    <%= render "shared/holding_status", row: row %>
  </div>

  <% if problem %>
    <div class="mt-2 pl-4 space-y-1" data-role="holding-detail">
      <% rules.each do |budget, due_on| %>
        <div class="flex justify-between text-xs text-gray-600 gap-4">
          <span><%= pool_rule_label(budget) %></span>
          <span><%= number_to_currency(budget.amount) %> · <%= due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
        </div>
      <% end %>

      <%# Expanded with no dated rule to show is a real state, not a corner: a savings goal may
          hold none at all, and a rate category goes :overdrawn on rules that never carry a date.
          An empty box under a red line would leave the loudest row on the screen as the only one
          explaining nothing.

          Two cases, and `rules.empty?` does NOT imply a rate rule exists: :overdrawn is guarded on
          the balance alone, so a category with NO rules at all reaches it, expands, and used to
          promise the user it "refills at its rate each period" while nothing refills it — a flat
          untruth on the loudest row on the screen.

          `category.budgets` rather than a count query: HomePresenter#categories eager-loads them. %>
      <% if rules.empty? %>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600">
          <% if category.budgets.any? %>
            No dated rules — this category refills at its rate each period.
          <% else %>
            No rules on this category — nothing fills it.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
